 "stdout": "> d <- data.frame(y = c(2.1, 3.4, 1.9, 4.2, 3.3, 2.8, 1.1, 2.4, 2.9, 3.2, 1.3, 2.2),\n>                 g = rep(c(\"été\", \"b \\\"q\\\"\"), each = 6), id = rep(1:6, 2))\n> t.test(y ~ g, data = d, mu = 1e-300)\n\n\tWelch Two Sample t-test\n\ndata:  y by g\nt = -1.556, df = 9.9934, p-value = 0.1508\nalternative hypothesis: true difference in means between group b \"q\" and group été is not equal to 1e-300\n95 percent confidence interval:\n -1.8646238  0.3312905\nsample estimates:\nmean in group b \"q\"   mean in group été \n           2.183333            2.950000 \n\n> cor.test(d$y, d$id, method = \"kendall\", exact = FALSE)\n\n\tKendall's rank correlation tau\n\ndata:  d$y and d$id\nz = 0.69561, p-value = 0.4867\nalternative hypothesis: true tau is not equal to 0\nsample estimates:\n      tau \n0.1589104 \n\n> chisq.test(matrix(c(1, 2, 3, 4), 2))\n\n\tPearson's Chi-squared test with Yates' continuity correction\n\ndata:  matrix(c(1, 2, 3, 4), 2)\nX-squared = 2.9348e-32, df = 1, p-value = 1\n\n> summary(aov(y ~ g + Error(factor(id)), data = d))\n\nError: factor(id)\n          Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value Pr(>F)\nResiduals  5  4.867  0.9733               \n\nError: Within\n          Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value Pr(>F)\ng          1  1.763  1.7633   3.648  0.114\nResiduals  5  2.417  0.4833               \n> by(d$y, d$g, mean)\nd$g: b \"q\"\n[1] 2.183333\n------------------------------------------------------------ \nd$g: été\n[1] 2.95\n> sapply(split(d$y, d$g), FUN = sd)\n    b \"q\"       été \n0.8424172 0.8642916 \n> vapply(split(d$y, d$g), median, numeric(1))\nb \"q\"   été \n 2.30  3.05 \n> aggregate(d$y, by = list(grp = d$g), FUN = \"var\")\n    grp         x\n1 b \"q\" 0.7096667\n2   été 0.7470000\n> tapply(d$y, d$g, function(v) mean(v))\n   b \"q\"      été \n2.183333 2.950000 \n> mean <- function(x, ...) base::mean(x, ...) + 0\n> aggregate(y ~ g, data = d, FUN = mean)\n      g        y\n1 b \"q\" 2.183333\n2   été 2.950000\n> fisher.test(matrix(c(3, 1, 1, 3), 2), alternative = \"greater\")\n\n\tFisher's Exact Test for Count Data\n\ndata:  matrix(c(3, 1, 1, 3), 2)\np-value = 0.2429\nalternative hypothesis: true odds ratio is greater than 1\n95 percent confidence interval:\n 0.3135693       Inf\nsample estimates:\nodds ratio \n  6.408309 \n\n> prop.test(0, 10)\n\n\t1-sample proportions test with continuity correction\n\ndata:  0 out of 10, null probability 0.5\nX-squared = 8.1, df = 1, p-value = 0.004427\nalternative hypothesis: true p is not equal to 0.5\n95 percent confidence interval:\n 0.0000000 0.3445372\nsample estimates:\np \n0 \n\n> summary(lm(y ~ 1, data = d))$coefficients\n            Estimate Std. Error t value     Pr(>|t|)\n(Intercept) 2.566667  0.2617926  9.8042 9.004921e-07\n> m <- lm(y ~ g, data = d)\n> r <- m\n> summary(r)\n\nCall:\nlm(formula = y ~ g, data = d)\n\nResiduals:\n    Min      1Q  Median      3Q     Max \n-1.0833 -0.8583  0.1167  0.5167  1.2500 \n\nCoefficients:\n            Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)    \n(Intercept)   2.1833     0.3484   6.267  9.3e-05 ***\ngété          0.7667     0.4927   1.556    0.151    \n---\nSignif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1\n\nResidual standard error: 0.8534 on 10 degrees of freedom\nMultiple R-squared:  0.1949,\tAdjusted R-squared:  0.1144 \nF-statistic: 2.421 on 1 and 10 DF,  p-value: 0.1508\n\n> anova(m, lm(y ~ 1, data = d))\nAnalysis of Variance Table\n\nModel 1: y ~ g\nModel 2: y ~ 1\n  Res.Df    RSS Df Sum of Sq      F Pr(>F)\n1     10 7.2833                           \n2     11 9.0467 -1   -1.7633 2.4211 0.1508\n> x <- c(a = Inf, b = 1)\n> stop(\"deliberate failure é\")\n",
